1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =譚開龍 (Kāi Lóng)=
2 CONT
2 CONT 譚開龍 (Kāi Lóng) was born in Changxing Village, Taishan County, Guangdong Province, Qing Dynasty Era. His birth name is known, but his marriage name / generational name was 譚開龍 (Kāi Lóng).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to my paternal eldest aunt (Xiao Ying Tan), my paternal grandfather had mentioned in the past that 譚開龍 (Kāi Lóng) and 譚開鳳 (Kāi Fèng) were identical twins. It is unknown what happened to his younger brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT 譚開龍 (Kāi Lóng) had three sons - 譚文楫 (Wén Jí) - my second great grandfather; 譚文懷 (Wén Huái) and 譚文樑 (Wén Liáng). His youngest son, 譚文樑 (Wén Liáng), was adopted by his younger brother, 譚開鳳 (Kāi Fèng). It is unknown what happened to 
2 CONC his middle and last son.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to a Canadian Immigration Document that I had found which I assumed to be 譚開龍 (Kāi Lóng)'s C.I.28 document from Wednesday, October 28, 1914, 譚開龍 (Kāi Lóng) arrived in New Westminster of British Columbia in 1882 by sailin
2 CONC g ship and was given/adopted the Chinese English romanized name - Tom Lung.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on my research, between 1882 and 1896, Tom Lung established a Chinese Hand Laundry business in British Columbia before moving east into Quebec, eventually setting in Montreal Chinatown between 1895-1896. Eventually, the Chin
2 CONC ese Hand Laundry business became known as Sing Tom Laundry / Sang Tom Laundry.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unknown what happened to Tom Lung, but I presume that he passed away in Montreal Chinatown and had his remains transported back to his ancestral village in Taishan County for ancestral worship.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''(Added on Saturday, April 13, 2024)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT My father told me that Tom Lung went back to China and died in the village (circa late-1930s to 1940s?). He should be buried in the local village cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father also said that his paternal grandmother (my great grandmother, Kun Fang Chen) met him before he passed and said that he used opium.
