1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Tom Tong Fat (譚松发)=
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Edward Tom''' (b. 1902 August 03 - d. 1935 May 05) was born as 譚松发 (Tán Tōng Fāt) in ChangXing (常興村), Jinkeng Township, Jiangmen, Xinning/Sunning (新寧) (currently known as Taishan County), Kwangtung Province (currently known as 
2 CONC Guangdong Province), Qing Dynasty Era (清朝時代).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family:==
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward's father, 譚文楫 (Tán Wén Jí), had two wives -【(李)】譚氏 and【(陳)】譚氏. (As of 2025 December 06, their names are unknown and will most likely be unknown.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward's older paternal half brother, 譚裔均 (Tán Yì Jūn) was born from the first wife【(李氏)】, and Edward was born to the second wife 【(陳)】. Their mother's names are unknown and it's unknown if they had sister(s) as only the male name
2 CONC s were recorded in the family genealogy book.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Other Names:==
2 CONT
2 CONT His birth name was 譚松发 (Tán Tōng Fāt), but his generational name was 譚裔益 (Tán Yì Yì) - Mandarin / Tom You Yick - Cantonese/Taishanese). His chosen English name was Edward Tom.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unknown how Tom Tong obtained the English name Edward Tom, but I speculated that. he mst have either chosen the name while attending the Montreal Chinese Missions Church, was given the name by someone within the church, or w
2 CONC as given the name shortly before he succumbed to tuberculosis on May 05, 1935, as a way to set roots.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Early Life:==
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Edward Tom's Canadian Immigration Records C.I.9., Edward emigrated to the Dominion of Canada in 1907 at the age of 4, presumably living with his paternal grandfather (Tom Lung), his father (Tom Wen Ji), and/or his fat
2 CONC her's younger sibling's side of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Wednesday, October 28, 1914 (Age 12), Edward left Montreal, took the Canadian Pacific Railway (C.P.R.) to Victoria, British Columbia, and returned to Kwangtung (Guangdong Province) via S.S. Talthybius, presumably with his pater
2 CONC nal grandfather, 譚開龍 (Kāi Lóng), who was also known as Tom Lung, to visit family or to meet his first wife, 李顕妣 (Xiǎn Bǐ). He returned to Victoria, British Columbia, Dominion of Canada, on Monday, September 27, 1915.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Saturday, April 22, 1916 (Age 14), Edward left Montreal, took the Canadian Pacific Railway (C.P.R) to Victoria, British Columbia, and returned to Kwangtung (Guangdong Province) via S.S. Titan. It is unknown if other family memb
2 CONC ers accompanied him on the trip journey, but his C.I.36 document states that he returned to Victoria, British Columbia, Dominion of Canada on Wednesday, December 12, 1917 (Age 15). It is unknown what his intentions were in returni
2 CONC ng to China, but it can be assumed that it would have been to either visit family or his family wanted him to meet a matchmaker.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tom Tong (Edward) left for Kwangtung (Guangdong Province) once or twice more before passing away from tuberculosis (assumed) on Tuesday, May 05, 1935.
2 CONT
2 CONT **According to my father, villagers from the Changxing Village who knew Edward when he was alive said that he might have been a weapons importer for the Kuomintang of China (中國民黨) during the Chinese Civil War (1927 - 1949), but my
2 CONC  father said when he visited Taishan, Guangdong Province several years ago, he heard from the villagers who knew my paternal great grandfather when he was alive that he was importing weapons from Montreal, Quebec Province, Canada 
2 CONC to China to fight off bandits in the 土樓 (tǔlóu) to protect the villagers.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Adult Life:==
2 CONT
2 CONT According to my paternal aunts, 譚小英 (Xiǎo Yīng) and 譚麗英 (Lì Yīng), Edward's first wife 李顕妣 (Xiǎn Bǐ) passed away from illness (circa 1918 - 1924) and their marriage didn't produce offspring.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward married his second wife, 陳坤芳 (Chén Kūn Fāng) between 1920 - 1928 and had two children - one daughter born between 1920 - 1924 named 譚瑞棠 (Ruì Táng), and a son born on July 21, 1928, named 譚沃榮 (Tán Wò Róng), who eventually be
2 CONC came my paternal grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on what I heard from my father and my paternal eldest aunt, 譚小英 (Tán Xiǎo Yīng), on the afternoon of Saturday, August 31, 2019, my paternal great grandfather, 譚裔益 (Tán Yì Yì) / 譚松发 (Tán Tōng Fāt) was transporting weapons fro
2 CONC m Montréal, Québec, Canada to Taishan, Guangdong Province, China to fight off bandits in the village in the 土樓 (tǔlóu). (This is what the people in the Taishan Village that he originated from said to my father.)
2 CONT
2 CONT During his time living in Montreal Chinatown, he used the name "Tom Tong Fat", which suggests that was his birth name. However, when he married, the elders of the village gave him the name - 譚裔益 (Yì Yì), with the generational char
2 CONC acter of 裔.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward left Jinkengcun, Taishan, Guangdong Province, China for Montreal, Quebec Province, Canada for work between 1928 - 1931, in which he operated a restaurant with his older half brother, 譚裔均 (Tán Yì Jūn), and might have been a 
2 CONC weapons importer for the Kuomintang of China (中國國民黨) during the Chinese Civil War (1927 - 1949).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Death:==
2 CONT
2 CONT According to documents provided by the Directeur de l'état civil of the Gouvernement du Québec, Edward Tom's last known address was 469 Rue Milton, Montreal, QC H2X 1W3, Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the documents provided by Medical Archivist, Aurélie Melançon of Grace Dart Hospital, Edward was admitted to Grace Dart Hospital on Friday, May 03, 1935, accompanied by someone named "J. M. Christie REJ" and passed aw
2 CONC ay on Sunday, May 05, 1935. His cause of death is unknown because Grace Dart Hospital either didn't keep records before 1936, but it can be assumed that he passed away from tuberculosis due to Grace Dart Hospital being a sanatoriu
2 CONC m for tuberculosis at the time of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was later interred at Mount Royal Cemetery on Tuesday, May 07, 1935, at Section N500-128.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grace Dart Hospital
2 CONT 6085 Sherbrooke Street East
2 CONT Montreal, QC H1M 2J4, Canada
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes:==
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Tom is currently buried in Mount Royal Cemetery in Section N 500 - 128.
2 CONT (1297 Foret Rd, Outremont, Quebec H2V 2P9, Canada)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==GPS Coordinates of Gravesite:==
2 CONT 45°30'22.0"N 73°36'12.7"W<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT 45.506100, -73.603529
2 CONT
