1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sam Lee Tom - 譚槐湛 (Tán Huái Zhàn): Lo Wah Que - A Long Time Overseas Chinese by Henry Tom'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam Lee Tom was born in Bak Sui (White Water) Taishan, in Guangdong Province, China in 1905, years after his father had returned from gold mining and working in San Diego, California. His dad died before he was 16 years old and he
2 CONC  left China with an older brother for India, where he learned carpentry and became a ship's carpenter, sailing around the world many times.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1932, Sam's ship came to New York City. Sam liked New York and he jumped ship and evaded capture by the immigration authorities and later became a legal resident and citizen. He was always proud that he did not have to
2 CONC  buy papers to come. He did buy papers for his bridge, Suey Sim Lee from Taishan, China., was Sam's picture bridge who arrived in New York in 1940 and married Sam, who she never met before. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam worked in Chinese wet wash laundries while Suey Sim ran a Chinese hand laundry and they lived in the rear of the laundry store and raised 5 children. Like so many other Chinese immigrants, Sam and Suey Sim made many sacrifices
2 CONC  so their children could be well educated and have a decent future.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam was a co-owner of a commercial Chinese Shirt pressing laundry name Scoy Laundry. This is where is worked 18-hour days, six and a half days a week and never had a real vacation in over 25 years. As his body started to fail him,
2 CONC  Sam sold the laundry and an old Italian friend sold him a small live chicken market which Sam operated for several years. Afterwards he operated a Chinese hand laundry for a few months before he had to retire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam passed away in 1987 after a lifetime of hard work and dedication to his wife and family.
